--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. INITIAL PLAN
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Language: C++ (required by rules.md). Direct POSIX TCP sockets. No Boost.Asio,
libevent, asyncio, or other forbidden frameworks.

Programs:
  1. Exchange server  — listen, accept many clients, match orders, notify
  2. Trader client    — LOGIN, BUY/SELL/CANCEL, print async BOUGHT/SOLD
  3. Market-data client — SUBSCRIBE/UNSUBSCRIBE, print async TRADE

Layout required by SPEC.md:
  server/run-server
  client/run-trader
  client/run-market-data
  src/
  Makefile (optional but useful)
  README.md

Design choices (allowed by the spec):
  - Concurrency: one thread per connected client + one mutex around exchange
    state. An idle client blocks only its own recv thread. Shared state
    (order IDs, book, usernames, subscriptions) is always taken under that
    mutex.
  - Matching: same instrument, opposite side, exact same price. Resting
    orders at a price are FIFO (oldest first). The spec does not name a
    secondary priority rule; FIFO is deterministic.
  - Role detection: no HELLO command exists. LOGIN binds Trader;
    SUBSCRIBE/UNSUBSCRIBE bind Market-Data. Cross-role commands return ERROR.
  - Notifications: while the mutex is held, collect (session, message)
    pairs with ORDER_ACCEPTED first, then fills. Release the mutex, then
    send. Each session has its own send mutex so two threads cannot
    interleave bytes on one TCP stream.
  - Disconnected traders: outstanding orders stay (weak owner pointer).
    Later fills still happen; BOUGHT/SOLD is skipped if the owner is gone;
    TRADE still goes to subscribers.
  - I/O: recv line-buffer per connection. send loops until the full
    "message\n" is written.
  - Launchers: POSIX sh using exec and "$@" .
  - Build: Makefile, C++17, -pthread, binaries in build/.
  - Verify on WSL (this host is Windows; the course VM is FreeBSD; both
    are POSIX enough for this code).

Test plan:
  login uniqueness, equal-price match, unequal-price no match, partial
  fill, cancel, MD TRADE, disconnect leaving orders, malformed input,
  split/coalesced TCP writes.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
3. WHAT WAS TRIED AND HOW THE WORK MOVED
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Step A — Read SPEC.md and rules.md end to end.
  Needed a complete picture before writing sockets code: newline framing,
  exact match price, trader disconnect does not cancel orders, TRADE never
  goes to traders, launchers must exec.

Step B — Start this log with the plan above, then write code against it.
  Did not invent extra protocol verbs. Clients print server lines as they
  arrive so asynchronous BOUGHT/SOLD/TRADE work.

Step C — Implement src/common.hpp and src/common.cpp first.
  Reason: framing and integer/instrument parsing are shared by all three
  programs. parse_i32 accepts only digits (rejects "+", "-", empty, overflow).
  LineReader keeps a per-connection buffer so a message can be split across
  recv() calls and several messages can arrive in one recv(). send_all loops
  on partial writes. SIGPIPE ignored; SO_NOSIGPIPE set when the OS has it
  (FreeBSD).

Step D — Implement src/server.cpp (session + book + accept loop).
  First draft stored market-data clients as raw Session* and then tried to
  wrap them in shared_ptr at TRADE time via a helper that returned nullptr.
  That would have silently dropped every TRADE. Caught while reviewing the
  draft, before relying on tests. Changed md_clients_ to
  map<Session*, shared_ptr<Session>> so TRADE has a live session to send on.

  Matching lives in Exchange::match: look up the opposite-side deque at the
  incoming price, FIFO, trade_qty = min(remainings), drop orders at 0.

Step E — Implement trader and market-data clients.
  Both connect, send one initial command (LOGIN / SUBSCRIBE from argv),
  then run_interactive_client: a recv thread prints lines, the main thread
  reads stdin and forwards protocol lines. This is not request/response
  locking; async notifications print whenever they arrive.

Step F — Makefile, README, launchers.
  Launchers were written with Unix LF (not CRLF) because a CRLF shebang
  breaks on FreeBSD. A short Python helper created them, then the helper
  was deleted so it is not part of the submission.

Step G — Build on WSL with g++ -std=c++17 -Wall -Wextra -pthread.
  First compile succeeded with no warnings. Windows native g++ (MSYS2)
  exists but would need Winsock; the assignment is POSIX, so WSL was the
  right compile target on this machine.

Step H — Temporary Python TCP driver (_proto_test.py) against ./server/run-server.
  Why Python for the driver: fastest way to control exact bytes on the
  socket (split LOGIN, two commands in one send) without fighting the
  interactive stdin clients. Results of the first run:

    ALL TESTS PASSED
      duplicate username rejected
      unequal prices did not match
      cancel of outstanding order
      partial fill 3 of 5 at price 10, then remaining cancel
      IMCT full fill; unsubscribed MD did not need that TRADE
      carol disconnect, bob hits her resting order, MD still got TRADE
      BUY before LOGIN / trader SUBSCRIBE / bad instrument / qty 0 /
        negative price / unknown command all ERROR, server still served dave
      split "LOG"+"IN eve\nBUY ..." parsed as two messages

  That file was deleted after the run so the tree matches the required
  submission layout (no extra test harness).

Step I — Review against "idle/slow client must not stall the exchange".
  LOGIN/CANCEL/SUBSCRIBE originally called send() while still holding the
  exchange mutex. A blocked write could freeze matching for everyone.
  Refactored those paths to build the response string under the lock and
  send after release. Order matching already used a PendingSend list +
  flush() after unlock. Session::alive became atomic to avoid a data race
  between the worker thread and matching.

Step J — Rebuild server, rerun the same protocol checks.
  ALL TESTS PASSED again. Makefile also chmod +x the three launchers on
  `make` so unzip-on-FreeBSD is less likely to leave them non-executable.

